  "overlay_description": "<p><b>Legend</b><br><img src=\"https://wyattleathorn7.github.io/great-lakes-live-environment/leaf_color/legend.png?v=leaf-hls-v3-80f3e9783243-r13\" width=\"600\" alt=\"legend\"><br>What each color means (one trip around the year, left to right on the key): <b>deep blue</b> = dormant winter; <b>light blue/cyan</b> = spring awakening and bud break; <b>teal-green</b> = leaf emergence; <b>green</b> = growing season; <b>yellow-green/yellow</b> = first color; <b>orange</b> = autumn coloring; <b>red/magenta</b> = peak color into leaf drop; <b>purple</b> = late drop returning to <b>deep-blue</b> dormant (the scale wraps: both ends are winter). Color is the satellite-derived growth stage, not the color of individual trees. Every vegetated land pixel is painted from the newest clear HLS observation in the rolling window; persistent gaps fall back to the most recent valid observation. <b>Transparent = cities, bare ground, open water, or non-vegetated land</b>.</p><p>NASA HLS harmonized Landsat/Sentinel-2 observations + derived phenology state</p><p><b>Units:</b> phenology color scale (circular)<br/><b>Source:</b> NASA HLS v2.0 Landsat 8/9 + Sentinel-2 via Planetary Computer (30 m)<br/><b>Update:</b> LATEST AVAILABLE COMPOSITE<br/><b>Provenance:</b> <a href=\"https://planetarycomputer.microsoft.com/dataset/hls2-l30\">https://planetarycomputer.microsoft.com/dataset/hls2-l30</a></p>"
